<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Khalq</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Khalq ('the masses') was the more radical of the two factions of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peoples-democratic-party-of-afghanistan">People's Democratic Party of Afghanistan</a>, formed when the party split in 1967. Led by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nur-muhammad-taraki">Nur Muhammad Taraki</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hafizullah-amin">Hafizullah Amin</a>, it drew support from Pashtun teachers and army officers and dominated the government after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saur-revolution">Saur Revolution</a> of 1978. The Soviet intervention of December 1979 put its rival faction, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/parcham">Parcham</a>, in power.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/khalq.jpg" alt="Khalq" width="320" height="301" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: UnknownUnknown , vectorisation by User:Orange Tuesday · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Emblem_of_Afghanistan_(1978-1980).svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
